\subsection{Sensor and model-error injection}

Inertial measurements include white noise, residual constant bias, and slow
bias drift; magnetometer errors additionally include hard iron, scale/cross-axis
error, misalignment, and slow drift (Table~\ref{tab:sim_constants}).  The
accelerometer truth uses random-walk bias while the estimator uses a slow
Gauss--Markov residual about its temperature calibration, deliberately
introducing bias-process mismatch.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Simulation timing and sensor-error levels.}
  \label{tab:sim_constants}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
    \toprule
    Quantity & Value & Notes \\
    \midrule
    IMU rate & \SI{200}{Hz} & prediction and accel update \\
    Magnetometer rate & \SI{25}{Hz} & asynchronous update \\
    $S$ pseudo-update period & $\tau$-scaled & \SIrange{5}{250}{ms} bounds \\
    Accel white noise & \SI{0.0148}{m.s^{-2}} & RMS per sample \\
    Accel bias RW & \SI{5e-4}{m.s^{-2}.s^{-0.5}} & truth model \\
    Gyro white noise & \SI{0.00157}{rad.s^{-1}} & RMS per sample \\
    Gyro bias RW & \SI{1e-5}{rad.s^{-1}.s^{-0.5}} & truth model \\
    Mag white noise & \SI{0.80}{\micro\tesla} & RMS per mag sample \\
    Mag hard-iron residual & \SI{2.0}{\micro\tesla} & half-range \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Temperature is fixed in the scored simulations; no independent thermal
calibration-error, lag, or hysteresis experiment is performed.  The
finite-$\tau_b$ residual is therefore a structural model and stability
assumption, while the empirical test concerns robustness to the injected
random-walk mismatch.

The principal study also uses the committed reference-point configuration, so
the implemented lever-arm model is not physically validated off-CoG.  The
records represent a surface-following inertial package rather than a particular
hull RAO.

\subsection{Paired Monte Carlo protocol}

Ten predeclared seed triplets vary wave phase, IMU noise/bias realization, and
sensor-error initialization.  Each triplet is shared by OU--II, OU--III, and
matched ablations, yielding within-seed paired differences.

The primary endpoint is the seed-level mean normalized vertical RMS displacement
error across the four stationary JONSWAP seas.  Scenario summaries report mean
and standard deviation over ten seeds.  Paired effects use percentile-bootstrap
intervals of seed-level differences; Student-$t$, exact sign, and exact paired
sign-flip tests provide companion checks for the single primary endpoint.
Secondary comparisons retain paired intervals without additional confirmatory
hypotheses.

A full simulator generation records replay source commit, implementation/build
hashes, input hashes, and normalized raw-row hash.  Statistical restatements are
permitted only while this dependency closure and raw rows match; the source
archive enforces equivalent file-hash guarantees when Git metadata is absent.

Metrics include $X$, $Y$, $Z$, and 3-D displacement RMS; roll, pitch, and yaw
RMS; accelerometer and gyroscope bias error; direction diagnostics; and
adaptation traces.  Thus vertical improvement cannot mask uncontrolled surge,
sway, or attitude error.

\subsection{Adaptation and covariance ablations}
\label{par:fixed-points}
\label{par:channel-ablation}

Three operating modes separate adaptation from architecture.  \emph{Adaptive}
uses the applied online schedule.  \emph{FixedNominal} freezes the point
calibrated from a noise-free $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$, $T_p=\SI{5.7}{s}$ record.
\emph{FixedOracle} applies the same calibration procedure to the scored sea, or
to the known final sea of the transition; it is scenario matched but not an
error-minimizing oracle.

A $2\times2$ OU--III channel ablation separates OU parameters from the integral
regularizer.  In \emph{$r_S$ only}, $(\tau,\sigma_{aw})$ are frozen while
$r_S$ adapts; in \emph{OU only}, the reverse applies.  A covariance-policy
control repeats Adaptive and FixedNominal without periodic latent-acceleration
covariance re-alignment.  These controls isolate mechanisms that otherwise
change simultaneously.

\subsection{Controlled non-stationary case}

The changing-sea study uses a $C^2$ quintic crossfade from
$(H_s,T_p)=(\SI{1.5}{m},\SI{5.7}{s})$ to
$(\SI{4.0}{m},\SI{11.4}{s})$ over $t=420$--$780$~s.  Endpoint records are
independently phase randomized, and derivative-consistent velocity and
acceleration include blend-derivative terms.  This is a kinematically closed
transition rather than a continuously evolving spectrum; both endpoint spectra
coexist during the blend, which can therefore be effectively bimodal.
Interpretation uses pure-start, blend, and pure-end, plus common trailing windows.

This case does not establish performance for arbitrary spectral evolution,
crossing seas, or continuously changing directional spectra.